+# Final review inv-2, cluster 19 (evidence-hg-lnw-4)
+
+## Scope and method
+
+Cluster holds five log files under evidence/hg-lnw.4/ (slices 000112 to 000116), all additions. The change is evidence only; no source is touched. Both SHAs verified as commits. The epic has no Requirement baseline section in its bd record, so the baseline paragraph does not apply.
+
+## What the logs show
+
+- focused.log is a red run: 49 passed, 1 failed, undo::tests::restore_preview_names_the_keyless_seat_window. The assertion saw the same settled-at-24h warning twice where one was expected. At head, src/undo/tests.rs expects exactly one such note, and full.log shows the lib suite green, so the red log is intermediate (red then green) evidence.
+- full.log: lib 1482 passed, 0 failed, plus every integration binary ok (one ignored test in one binary).
+- undo.log: 62 passed, 0 failed.
+- final-lib-repeat.log: three consecutive lib runs, 1482 passed each.
+- final-lib.log: 1481 passed, 1 failed. daemon::lock::tests::is_held_reports_holder failed on `assert!(!is_held(&p))` after dropping the lock. That file is not touched by the epic diff. The three clean repeats and full.log indicate a load-sensitive flake of an unrelated lock probe, not a regression from this cluster.
+
+## Assessment
+
+The cluster is correctly scoped as run evidence. The one red log that remains in the tree (final-lib.log) has no note explaining it, but the repeat log directly follows it and is green, so a reader can reconstruct the story. Nothing here lowers or misreports a spec requirement.
+
+## Verdict
+
+ready
+
+## Must fix before landing
+
+- none
+
+## Untested scope
+
+- none
+
+## Deferred OK
+
+- daemon::lock::tests::is_held_reports_holder failed once under full parallel load (final-lib.log line 1505) and passed in four later runs; worth a follow-up bead to find out whether a forked child inherits the flock fd, outside this epic.
+- final-lib.log carries no annotation that its single failure is a known flake; a one-line note in the evidence would help later readers.
